structure:
root directory    /
/bin                       //essential user commands
/sbin                      //system admin commands
/lib                       //shared libraries and Kernel modules
/etc                       //system configuration files
/usr                       //user applications and system resources
/opt                       //stands for Optional Application Directory, third party applications store their files here
/boot                      //boot loader files or GRUB2 in Red Hat Enterprise Linux
/root                      //root user's home directory
/home                      //regular users' home directories
/mnt                       //temporary mounted file systems
/srv                       //service data directories
/var                       //variable runtime data which can be modified while the system is running such as logs
/tmp                       
/dev                       //device files for hardware access
/proc                      //virtual files showing kernel state


commands:
$ ls *x*                   //list files which names or extensions have character 'x' 
$ ls file?                 //list files which names have 5 characters with 'file' at the start
$ ls ????                  //list files which names have 4 characters
$ ls [a-z]*                //list files which names start with a character
$ ls [!a]*                 //list files which names dont start with 'a'
$ ls *.{log,txt}           //list files which extension is .log or .txt
$ ls .*                    //list hidden files
$ tail                     //displays last 10 lines of a file, -n will set the number of lines displayed
$ head                     //displays first 10 lines of a file, -n will set the number of lines displayed
$ more                     //displays file contents, one page at a time
$ mkdir -p abc/def         //makes a directory abc which has another directory def inside
$ tree                     //shows the directory structure in the current directory
$ cp file1.txt abc/file2.txt          //copy file1 to abc directory and name it as file2
$ cp -r abc/ xyz           //copy abc directory recursively (including all directories and contents inside) to directory xyz
$ mv file1.txt file2.txt   //rename file1 to file2



file structure:
filename
   ↓
 inode
   ↓
file data
the inode is a database that describes the files or directory attributes, such as metadata and the physicacl location on the hard drive.
links:
hard links: a synced carbon copy of a file that refers directly to the inode of a file, it points to the same storage space or to the same inode where the content is present.
            it's the same file in different locations, it does modify the other file if it's updated.
$ ln file1.txt hard_link_file1          //create a hard link
$ ls -i                                 //show the inode
17656493   file1.txt      17656493   hard_link_file1        //output: both the file name and the hard link point to the same inode    
symbolic links: shortcut
& ln -s file2.txt symbolic_link_file2   //create a symbolic link
note: we cant create a hard link to a directory, but we can create a symbolic link to it, because a hard link needs to point to an inode, a directory is just used for structure in Linux,
      it doesnt hold contents so no inode is associated with it. However, a symbolic link can refer to either a directory or a file.



archive, compress using TAR:
$ tar -options archive_file files               //archive multiple files to archive_file
$ tar -cvf file=/abc/def/archive.tar file1      //eg.
$ tar -tvf /abc/def/archive.tar                 //list the contents of archive.tar, -t is to view contents
$ tar -xvf /abc/def/archive.tar                 //extract cotemts in the current directory
$ tar -xvf /abc/def/archive.tar -C /xyz/        //extract cotemts to a different directory







